{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman who played a significant role during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born in the year 1140 and died on March 28 1216. Raymond belonged to the House of Bures and was the second son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His brother Bohemond III died without a male heir and Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli through his sister’s lineage.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Career**\nRaymond’s early life began under the rule of his father Raymond II who was an ally of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond’s mother Hodierna was the sister of King Fulk of Jerusalem and Raymond’s uncle was King Amalric I. He was raised in the feudal traditions of the Crusader states where he learned about knighthood politics and warfare. Raymond initially served as a knight but his ambitions grew as he witnessed the shifting power dynamics in the region. His rise to prominence came after the death of Bohemond III in 1174. Bohemond had no sons so Raymond’s sister Maria was set to inherit Tripoli. However Bohemond’s widow Euphemia remarried Raymond’s younger brother Bohemond IV who later became Prince of Antioch. Raymond’s claim to Tripoli was through his sister and he became the guardian of his young nephew Bohemond IV after Bohemond III’s death.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Political Conflicts and Leadership**\nRaymond was involved in several political conflicts particularly with the Kingdom of Jerusalem which was ruled by Raymond’s cousin Guy of Lusignan and later by his brother-in-law Aimery of Lusignan. Raymond’s power base was in Tripoli and he often clashed with the Jerusalemite rulers over matters of jurisdiction and resources. His leadership became crucial during the siege of Acre in 1189–1191 which was a pivotal moment in the Third Crusade. Raymond along with the forces of Richard the Lionheart King of England and Philip II King of France played a key role in relieving the siege and securing a victory. His strategic contributions earned him respect among the Crusader leaders despite his political rivalries.\n\nRaymond’s reign was marked by efforts to strengthen Tripoli’s defenses against threats from Zengid and Ayyubid forces. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations with Muslim rulers such as Saladin. Raymond’s political maneuvering included alliances with the Knights Templar and Hospitallers who were influential military orders in the region. His policies aimed at maintaining the independence of Tripoli from the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nIn his later years Raymond faced challenges from his nephew Bohemond IV who sought to reclaim Tripoli. Raymond’s health declined and he struggled to maintain control over the county. He died in 1216 after a long reign that saw significant political intrigue and military campaigns. Raymond’s legacy lies in his ability to navigate the complex power structures of the Crusader states and his contributions to the defense of Tripoli. His life reflects the struggles of the Crusader nobles who balanced loyalty to the Holy Land with personal ambitions and regional politics.\n\nRaymond III’s story is a part of the broader history of the Crusades and the dynamic interactions between Christian and Muslim powers in the medieval Levant. His reign highlights the importance of local leadership in the survival of the Crusader states against formidable adversaries. The County of Tripoli under Raymond’s rule remained a key player in the politics and warfare of the region for several decades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of"